# Three-model Prime replay — synthesis

Date: 2026-10-30. All three submissions retained: Codex 0172, Grok 0173, Claude 0174.
Twelve attempted routes, four per model. Six assisted mechanical routes are partial;
the substantive review and next-pack/no-pack outputs survive. No general assistance
benefit or model ranking can be measured from this first delivery.

## What stayed consistent
All three see useful original benchmark work but require the later method controls
before actual comparison. All three avoid an unnecessary packet-helper feature
build. Each separates mechanical custody from judgment and respects the failed
reader rather than inventing receipt bindings. Failed dependency checks stay visible.

## What differed and matters
Claude's second/manual case-B pass found 19 omitted nested evidence files; Codex
and Grok missed them because their top-level artifact checks passed. Prime verified
all original files recoverable at the historical cut. This is a preparation omission,
not lost Builder work. Good source selection is still intelligence's responsibility.

Completion labels differed because one model assessed the first bounded build and
another assessed broader integration. Bind each result to its stated goal and
scope before comparing labels. Do not normalize away the disagreement.

Grok needed a Director-carried Prime checkpoint. It supplied no subject answer,
but remains a method intervention. Grok's long elapsed run does not establish
repeated tool misuse. Its journal also exposes overlapping day snapshots and exit-
code layers, which must be distinguished in later measurements.

## Historical comparison
The original benchmark review retained the foundation, then commissioned controls.
The subsequent 0166 review accepted those controls with 31 revised tests and a
repeated drift probe. The replay proposals mostly recover that same engineering
direction, but their historical packs must not duplicate that completed repair.
The original packet-helper review accepted bounded use and warned about historical
versus live observations. Claude adds a finding about what this replay actually
delivered; Prime resolved its provenance without changing original sources.

## What compute did and did not do
Packets verified custody, journals retained refusals and records refused absent
source identity. None supplied semantic findings. Default packet coverage was too
thin for the intended review; the standalone dependency and evidence closure was
incomplete. A successful subprocess or readable journal is not completed work.
The original smoke check tested allowed exit/file presence, not usable state.

## Repair work
0515/05.03: bounded adapter diagnostics and observation-failure state.
0516/05.01: exact runtime/nested-evidence closure, minimum evidence, defined fallback,
actual-delivery readiness checks and a separately identified second replay kit.
0411 readiness proposal: stable process obligations; later focused law alignment
0036–0039 after working behavior is demonstrated. No broad kernel rebuild indicated.

Both drafts now have all participant findings. Final execution source pins,
implementer/capture allocations and a named release are still explicit preparation
steps; no build has started. Packaging must use a stable accepted adapter cut and
must not race with edits. Preserve first-pass evidence and unknown usage/cost.
